WORKFLOW (executed, script inspect_subjects.py, output in inspect_output.txt):
1) Loaded the main CSV (927 rows × 57 cols; 141 unique O/R-pair ids; one row per original–replication-lab combination; lab_id: 49 labs). The second CSV (143 rows) has no subject-type columns and was not usable for this question.
2) Value counts: subjects.o — students 737, anyone 143, community 46, NA 1; subjects.r — students 744, online 148, community 25, anyone 9, NA 1; discipline — Social 668, Cognitive 241, Economics 18 rows; project — ml1 592, ml3 220, rpp 97, ee 18.
3) Student proportions by discipline (row-level / unique-study level): Social 82.04%/83.78% (original), 78.44%/81.08% (replication); Cognitive 71.37%/75.51%, 83.82%/81.63%; Economics 94.44%/94.44%, 100%/100%. By project: ee 94.4%/100%, ml1 75.0%/72.97–100%, ml3 90%/100%, rpp 80.41%/76.29% — no project matches both targets.
4) Aggregation variants tested for exact reproduction of 82.4%/80.9% (all reported for Social / Cognitive / Economics):
   A) row-level with both subject fields non-NA: Social 82.16%/78.56%;
   B) majority vote across labs per id (study-level): Social 83.78%/81.08%;
   C) excluding rows where aggregated==True: Social row 81.97%/77.81%, study-level 83.78%/81.08%;
   D) first lab row per id: Social 83.78%/81.08%.
   NO variant reproduces 82.4%/80.9% exactly (closest gaps: original −0.24 pp…+1.38 pp; replication −2.46…+0.18 pp), and a full discipline×project subset scan found no subset matching either pair. The conclusion is UNCHANGED under every variant: Social is the only domain close to both targets (original ≈82–84% vs Cognitive 71–76% and Economics 94.4%; replication ≈78–81% vs Cognitive 82–84% and Economics 100%); Economics and Cognitive each fail one side decisively in all variants.

